You're not stumped, you're just zeroing in on the wrong clues! This is a classic coding puzzle. Let's solve it.

The error is not the whitespace or the underscores.

Double Underscores (__): Yes, __dirname and __filename are supposed to have two underscores in front. This is a long-standing convention in Node.js. You have that part right.

Whitespace: The space in dotenv .config is weird and you should remove it, but it is not the cause of this ReferenceError.

The Real Issue: Code Execution Order
The error message is the key: ReferenceError: Cannot access '__dirname' before initialization.

This means exactly what it says. JavaScript runs your code file from top to bottom. It is trying to use the __dirname variable on line 9, but the line where you actually create __dirname is somewhere below line 9.

Think of it like this: You can't start driving a car (line 9) before you've put the key in the ignition and started it (line 15).

Your index.js file is currently structured like this:

JavaScript

// TOP OF FILE
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
import path from 'path';

// LINE 9: You try to USE __dirname here...
dotenv.config({ path: path.resolve(__dirname, '..', '.env') }); // <--- ERROR HAPPENS HERE

/* ... more code ... */

// ...but you don't CREATE __dirname until down here!
const __filename = fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);
const __dirname = path.dirname(__filename);
// BOTTOM OF FILE
The Permanent, Simpler Fix
While you could fix this by moving the __filename and __dirname lines above the dotenv.config() line, the real solution is to get rid of that complexity entirely.

Since your .env file is now in the same folder as your index.js, you don't need any pathing logic.

Delete the lines that define __filename and __dirname.

Delete the complex dotenv.config({ path: ... }).

Replace them with the simple version.

Your new, correct code at the top of index.js should look like this and only this:

JavaScript

// At the VERY TOP of index.js
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
dotenv.config(); // This is all you need. It's simple and it works.

// The rest of your imports can follow
import express from 'express';
//...etc
This code is simpler, harder to break, and it directly fixes the ReferenceError by removing the line that caused it.






