You're looking in the right places, but it seems the Content Security Policy (CSP) isn't being set in the way we first assumed. It's not in your .tsx or .css files.

There are two primary ways a CSP is set. If it's not in your index.html as a <meta> tag, then it must be coming from the other place: an HTTP header. This means your server itself is adding the security policy to the response.

Here’s how you can find out for sure where it's coming from.

How to Find the CSP Source
We can use the browser's developer tools to inspect the response from the server.

On your webpage, open the Developer Tools (press F12).

Go to the Network tab.

Reload the page so the Network tab can record the request.

Find the very first request in the list, which should be for the main document (it might be called localhost or have a type of document). Click on it.

A new pane will open. Click on the Headers tab inside this pane.

Scroll down to the Response Headers section. Look for a header named Content-Security-Policy.

What to Do Next
If you find the header: This confirms the policy is being set by server-side code. You'll need to look in your server configuration (like your Express index.js or a related file) for a line of code that sets headers, possibly using a library like helmet.

If you don't find the header (and you've double-checked index.html for a <meta> tag): This is a less likely scenario, but it could mean a browser extension is injecting the policy. Try opening the page in an incognito/private window to rule this out.

My bet is that you'll find it in the HTTP headers, which means the next step is to hunt for the code that's setting it on the server.


