It's a black screen with white letters because the browser can no longer find your CSS file. Moving the HTML file broke the path to your stylesheet.

Why It Broke: The Treasure Map Problem
Think of your index.html file as a treasure map. On that map, you have instructions like, "To find the styles, look in the styles folder right next to me." This is called a relative path.

When you moved the map (your index.html file) to a new folder, the old instructions don't work anymore. The browser follows the old path from the new location and can't find the CSS file, so you get an unstyled page.

How to Fix It
You need to update the paths in your index.html to reflect its new location.

Step 1: Find the Broken Paths
You can confirm this is the problem in your browser:

Open the Developer Tools (F12).

Look at the Console tab. You will almost certainly see red 404 (Not Found) errors for your CSS file (index.css) and possibly other files.

Step 2: Correct the Paths in Your HTML
Now, open your index.html file and find the <link> tag for your stylesheet.

HTML

<link rel="stylesheet" href="/index.css">
You need to adjust that href path.

If you moved your index.html into a subfolder (like from frontend/ to frontend/pages/), you need to tell it to go "up" one level first. You do this with ../.

For example, href="/index.css" might need to become href="../index.css".

You'll need to do the same for any <script> tags or <img> tags whose paths are now also broken.






